      "utf8": "you get back to where you started pretty\nmuch by definition so presumably quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610000,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information is conserved in this type of\ntime rehearsal. Mathematically, the particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615180,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a rewinding universe actually look\nlike they underwent a charge parity inversion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620760,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Matter that was going forward\nin time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622620,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looks like parity flipped antimatter\ngoing backwards in time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626960,
    "dDurationMs": 4239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This interpretation of antimatter as time\nreverse matter was first proposed by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631200,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ernst Stueckelberg in 1941 but is now largely\nassociated with Richard Feynman - it's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636600,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "essential to his path integral approach\nto quantum mechanics and to Feynman"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640600,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diagrams maybe that's why he was so into\nbuilding antimatter clocks. So, yeah the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645280,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe is not symmetric under this\nsimple version of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "T reversal. It's the precise inverse of a\nCP transformation, the two undo each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654280,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do a CP transformation and then a simple\nT transformation and you get back to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where you started. If CP is violated then\nthis simple time reversal is also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664080,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "violated and we see this violation in\nthe asymmetry between matter and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669090,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antimatter\nthen there's the whole entropy business"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672060,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although it's connection to quantum\nmechanics is still not well understood"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675660,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like I said this simple interpretation\nof T as rewinding the universe is not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681720,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what we usually mean by the T in CPT -\nthat T is more accurately thought of as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688290,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flipping the direction of the evolution\nof a physical system - an explosion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693240,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes an implosion and particle decay\nbecomes particle creation. You're not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697770,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rewinding time, you're not converting\nmatter to antimatter - you're just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702000,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reversing all momentum and spin.\nEssentially you're taking all particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the universe and pointing them back\nin the direction they came from. If the T"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in CPT is conserved then after\nreversing all particle motion those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715840,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles should perfectly retrace\ntheir steps and perfectly reverse all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720150,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reactions in their histories. As this\nmotion reverse universe evolves forward"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725190,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in time it should end up back in its\nstarting configuration. On the other hand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729950,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "broken T symmetry says that if you do\nthis reversal the future won't perfectly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736380,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mirror the past. One prediction of this T\nsymmetry is that all processes should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740730,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take the same amount of time going\nforwards as backwards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743850,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example a quantum transition between\none particle type and another should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748620,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take the same time in either direction\nand that gives us a test: in 2012"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754580,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists from the BaBar collaboration\nat the Stanford Linear Accelerator"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758610,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Center tested the speed with which B\nmaisons transition between two types in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765510,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a T symmetric universe the speeds should\nbe the same in either direction they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771720,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were not the same\nreversing the direction of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774790,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interaction changed something fundamental\nabout the physics indicating a violation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780160,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of T symmetry. But remember, killing T\nsymmetry is a good thing because it may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785860,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "save CPT symmetry. The last 70\nyears have been a true roller coaster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791200,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ride for the symmetries of nature as\nexperiments found broken symmetries one,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795580,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by one the first parity then charge\nparity the hallow'd CPT symmetry looked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801460,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in danger unless we gave up on the\nsymmetry of time itself but now with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807130,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time reversal symmetry also looking like\nit's broken the CPT theorem looks safe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812840,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Feynman's mirror reflected antimatter\nclock will work just fine but as well as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817920,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ticking backwards every atom every\nsubatomic particle needs to tick"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822190,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "backwards also so we've discovered our\nperfect mirror universe it's a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827530,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reflection of all three: charge, \nspace, time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832600,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone, I'm back from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834040,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon,\naka. Australia, which as you know is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837760,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CPT inverted version of Canada. The last\nepisode before the break was, \"Why string"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843580,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory is Wrong\". Today, I want to address\nthe comments to that episode or catch up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848740,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on last week's comments next time - in\nfact, I really want to address a few"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853270,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "points made by FieldStrength on the PBS\nSpace Time subreddit: they covered all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859300,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most important points, now first I\nwant to clarify that despite the title,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865570,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"Why String Theory is Wrong\", the point of the\nvideo was not to argue that string"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869770,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory is wrong instead it was a playful\nfollow-up to why string theory is right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874660,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'll reiterate my real opinion on the\nmatter after addressing fill strengths"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879430,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "specific than very reasonable criticisms.\nIn the video we note that string theory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884920,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty much requires supersymmetry in\norder to be right and that supersymmetry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889990,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hasn't yet been detected at the expected\nenergy scale that's the energy scale"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895120,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expected if supersymmetry is to also\nprovide a neat resolution to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900280,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standard models hierarchy problem but\nstring theory doesn't require"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904690,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supersymmetry to be at that\nscale at all for the purpose of string"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909340,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory supersymmetric particles could be\nfar beyond the energies detected by the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913720,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Large Hadron Collider - so FieldStrength\nargues that our current non detection of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919600,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supersymmetric particles is no\nfalsification of string theory and, of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924070,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "course, that's correct but I didn't say\notherwise - apologies if it seemed implied."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929740,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, I do argue that this non\ndetection is a bit of a hiccup"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934650,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supersymmetry is highly compelling if it\nprovides an answer to the hierarchy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem and gives us super string theory\nit will be odd if supersymmetry existed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945160,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as part of super string theory but did\nnothing to help with the hierarchy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948850,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem because it was at far too high\nan energy scale. But then as I did"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953830,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mention in the video the current Large\nHadron Collider results do not yet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958000,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "completely rule out all energy scales\nuseful for the hierarchy problem only at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963440,
    "dDurationMs": 2651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the energy scales that solved the\nproblem most neatly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966091,
    "dDurationMs": 5149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "FieldStrength's other point is that the large undefined\nparameter space of string theory -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the so called 'String landscape' is no more of a\nproblem for string theory than it is for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976360,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the standard model - I totally agree. In\nfact it's less of a problem for string"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980860,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory because in the standard model\nmany parameters are hopelessly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985280,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unconstrained by the theory itself while\nin string theory it's arguable that only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990130,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one parameter is hopelessly\nunconstrained. My point is not that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994300,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "string theory is more wrong in the\nstandard model it's just that it's not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997840,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yet \"right\" - \nin this sense. To quote me it was never"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002580,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really right and to quote Peter Weitz\nquoting Pauli, \"it's not even wrong\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point being that while not wrong there's\nnot yet a way to properly test his"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rightness or wrongness. FieldStrength's\nfinal point is that the untestability"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019920,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of string theory is connected to the\nextreme energy scale of quantum gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024600,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that problem is not unique to string\ntheory. Now we do talk about that in our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030270,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first episode on general quantum gravity\nso apologies for not repeating it here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035640,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So yeah string theory is apparently not\nyet testable,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039180,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but I disagree with those who say that\nthis type of untestability means that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043920,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the field is not science. String theory\nmay be currently untestable due to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049740,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy scales involved but the universe\nhas no obligation to make itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054150,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "currently testable to any particular\nparticle collider building technology"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058620,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "level. It's under no obligation to make\nevery next layer of reality accessible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063870,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the next generation of physicists. We\nblasted through several reality layers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069300,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from atoms to quantum fields in the past\nhundred years or so but maybe the next"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074070,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer will take another thousand years\nor more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076290,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Randy Copeland brilliantly notes that\nphysicists are a bit like 'House' M.D."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081300,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trying to find a diagnosis that fits the\nsymptoms with the symptoms being all of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086340,
    "dDurationMs": 4589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reality itself.\nNice observation also makes me feel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090929,
    "dDurationMs": 3691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better when I put my title down when I\nbuy airline tickets in the hope of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094620,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting an upgrade. I'm still dreading\nthe day when the crew come to me because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098670,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's some medical emergency on board\nand I have to tell them what type of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102300,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doctor I am."
    } ]
  } ]
}
